I have discovered that grouping my data and scaling them significantly improves model performance, but takes my previously nicely normally distributed response variable and creates a distribution that is more peaked on the left and skewed to the right.

It is my understanding that the family argument of the INLA call refers to the conditional distribution f(Y|X). Does the marginal distribution of the response variable matter at all? Would it be beneficial to apply an additional transformation so that the marginal distribution is normal again?

Thanks

